      "text": "We begin by noting that the absolute value function $|n + 6|$ can be expressed piecewise as:\n\n\\[\n|n + 6| = \\begin{cases} \nn + 6 & \\text{if } n + 6 \\geq 0 \\\\\n-(n + 6) & \\text{if } n + 6 < 0 \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ |n + 6| = \\begin{cases} \nn + 6 & \\text{if } n \\geq -6 \\\\\n-n - 6 & \\text{if } n < -6 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nGiven the equation $|n + 6| = 2 - n$, we need to consider the two cases based on the expressions for $|n + 6|$.\n\n**Case 1:** $n \\geq -6$\nIn this case, $|n + 6| = n + 6$. Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nSolving for $n$, we add $n$ to both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\nWe now check if $n = -2$ satisfies the condition $n \\geq -6$. Indeed, $-2 \\geq -6$, so $n = -2$ is a valid solution in this case.\n\n**Case 2:** $n < -6$\nIn this case, $|n + 6| = -(n + 6)$. Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nAdding $n$ to both sides:\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, as $-6$ does not equal $2$. Therefore, there are no solutions in this case.\n\nCombining the results from both cases, we conclude that the only solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
