      "text": "Let's solve the equation step by step. The given equation is:\n\n\\[\n|n + 6| = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nThe absolute value function \\( |x| \\) can be written as:\n\n\\[\n|n + 6| = \n\\begin{cases} \nn + 6 & \\text{if } n + 6 \\geq 0 \\\\\n-(n + 6) & \\text{if } n + 6 < 0 \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two separate cases to consider:\n\n**Case 1:** \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\n\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). Substituting this into the original equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\nNext, subtract 6 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\n\nThen, divide by 2:\n\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for Case 1.\n\n**Case 2:** \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\n\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) \\). Substituting this into the original equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, meaning there are no solutions in this case.\n\nCombining the results from both cases, the only valid solution is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
