      "text": "We separate the absolute value on the left into two cases based on its definition: $n + 6 = 2 - n$ and $n + 6 = -(2 - n)$.\nFrom $n + 6 = 2 - n$, we get $2n = -4 \\Rightarrow n = -2$.\nFrom $n + 6 = -(2 - n)$, we get $n + 6 = -2 + n \\Rightarrow 6 = -2$, which is a contradiction, so there are no other solutions to this equation.\nHence, the solution is $\\boxed{n = -2}$.\nThis problem tests understanding of absolute value and basic algebra.",
